'''water-shield''' (wâ′tėr-shēld), ''n.'' A plant of either of the genera ''Cabomba'' and ''Brasenia'', which form the suborder ''Cabombeæ'', of the ''Nympheaceæ:'' so called as consisting of aquatics with peltate leaves. {{smaller|''Brasenia peltata'', with floating oval leaves 1 to 4 inches across and small dull-purple flowers, is found in North America, Asia, Africa, and Australia. Also ''water-buckler''.}}

'''water-shoot''' (wâ′tėr-shöt), ''n.'' [< ''water'' + ''shoot'', prob. confused also with ''chute''.] '''1.''' A pipe or trough for discharging water from a building. —'''2†.''' A shoot from the root of a tree.

'''water-shrew''' (wâ′tėr-shrö), ''n.'' An oar-footed aquatic shrew. {{smaller|In Europe the best-known species is ''Crossopus fodiens''. The corresponding American species is ''Neosorex palustris''. See second cut under ''shrew''.}}

